        "groqFailed": false,
        "answer": "No – a landlord cannot just walk into your rental without giving you notice. Under the Residential Tenancies Act, they must provide at least 24 hours’ written notice before entering, unless one of two exceptions applies:\n\n1. **Emergency** – a situation that threatens health or safety (e.g., a burst pipe).  \n2. **Your consent at the time of entry** – you agree to let them in then and there.\n\nIf neither exception applies, the notice must state a legitimate reason such as a repair, an inspection by a mortgagee/insurer, or another reason listed in your tenancy agreement [1].\n\n**What to do if it happens:**  \n- Politely remind the landlord of the 24‑hour notice requirement and ask for written notice in the future.  \n- Keep a record of any un‑notified entries (dates, times, what was done).  \n- If the behavior continues, you can contact the Landlord and Tenant Board or the Rental Housing Enforcement Unit for assistance.  \n\nDocumenting everything and communicating in writing will help protect your rights.\n\n_Note: This is general information, not legal advice. For landlord-tenant disputes, contact Waterloo Region Community Legal Services or the Landlord and Tenant Board directly._",
