          {
            "tool": "escalate_ticket",
            "args": {
              "reason": "High priority: no heat in winter and landlord unresponsive since December",
              "ticketId": "T-2f4c77a4"
            },
            "error": null,
            "priority": null
          },
          {
            "tool": "draft_followup_email",
            "args": {
              "body": "Dear Off-Campus Housing Office,\n\nI am writing regarding my ongoing issue with a broken heater in my rental unit. I have sent three emails to my landlord since December, but have received no response. As winter temperatures are low, the lack of heating poses a serious health and safety concern.\n\nCould you please intervene on my behalf to ensure the heater is repaired promptly, or advise on next steps I can take to address this unresponsive landlord?\n\nThank you for your assistance.\n\nSincerely,\n[Student Name]",
              "office": "off_campus_housing",
              "subject": "Urgent assistance needed for broken heater",
              "ticketId": "T-2f4c77a4"
            },
            "error": null,
            "priority": null
          }
        ],
        "criticTools": [],
        "ticketsCreated": 1,
        "groqFailed": false,
        "answer": "I’m sorry you’re dealing with this – a broken heater in the middle of winter is stressful, especially when you’ve already tried reaching out several times.\n\n**Here’s a practical, step‑by‑step plan you can follow right away**\n\n1. **Send a formal written notice**  \n   - Draft a clear letter or email that describes the problem (broken heater), references the three previous emails you sent, and states a reasonable deadline for the repair (e.g., “please fix the heater by [date, 7‑10 days from today]”).  \n   - Keep a copy for yourself and, if you email, request a read‑receipt or follow up to confirm it was received.  \n   - This is the first step recommended by the university’s off‑campus housing guidance and the Residential Tenancies Act [1].\n\n2. **If the deadline passes with no action…**  \n   - **Contact the Landlord and Tenant Board (LTB).** You can file an application for dispute resolution to compel the repair and, if needed, seek compensation for any loss of use or increased heating costs.  \n   - The LTB is the official body that handles maintenance‑related disputes once the landlord has been given proper notice [1].\n\n3. **If the heater issue is an immediate health or safety risk** (e.g., you have no heat at all and the temperature is unsafe):  \n   - Call the **Rental Housing Enforcement Unit** of the Ministry of Municipal Affairs and Housing right away. They can intervene in urgent cases where a vital service is missing.  \n   - You can also reach out to **UW Campus Police (non‑emergency)** for advice on safety concerns, or the **WUSA Student Legal Protection Program** for free legal advice and possible representation [FAQ].\n\n4. **Document everything**  \n   - Save copies of all emails, the written notice, any responses, and photos of the heater (or lack of heat).  \n   - A clear paper trail strengthens your case with the LTB or any enforcement agency [FAQ].\n\n5. **Consider additional support**  \n   - The **WUSA Student Legal Services** team can review your situation, help you draft the notice, and advise on the LTB process. You can email them at the address listed in the FAQ.  \n   - If you need immediate advice, the **Rental Housing Enforcement Unit** and **UW Campus Police** are the go‑to contacts for urgent safety issues.\n\n**Quick checklist**\n\n- ☐ Write a formal notice with a specific repair deadline.  \n- ☐ Keep a copy and proof of delivery.  \n- ☐ If no fix by the deadline, file a claim with the LTB.  \n- ☐ For urgent safety concerns, call the Rental Housing Enforcement Unit (or UW Campus Police for safety advice).  \n- ☐ Reach out to WUSA Legal Protection for free student legal help.\n\nYou have clear rights under the Residential Tenancies Act: the landlord must keep the unit in a good state of repair and fit for habitation [1][3]. Following these steps will help you enforce those rights while keeping everything documented and professional. Good luck, and let me know if you need help drafting the notice or navigating the LTB process!\n\n_Note: This is general information, not legal advice. For landlord-tenant disputes, contact Waterloo Region Community Legal Services or the Landlord and Tenant Board directly._",
