          {
            "tool": "draft_followup_email",
            "args": {
              "body": "Dear Off‑Campus Housing Office,\n\nI am writing regarding my off‑campus residence where the heater has been non‑functional for the past week. With winter temperatures dropping, the lack of heat is creating an unsafe living condition. I have attempted to contact my landlord but have not received a resolution.\n\nCould you please intervene on my behalf to ensure the heater is repaired promptly? I appreciate your assistance in addressing this urgent maintenance issue.\n\nThank you,\n[Student Name]\n[Student ID]\n[Apartment Address]",
              "office": "off_campus_housing",
              "subject": "Urgent: Heater Not Working for One Week",
              "ticketId": "T-01ec4604"
            },
            "error": null,
            "priority": null
          }
        ],
        "criticTools": [],
        "ticketsCreated": 1,
        "groqFailed": false,
        "answer": "I’m sorry I can’t send the email for you, but here’s a concise, polite draft you can copy‑paste and send to your landlord. It follows the two‑step escalation plan recommended by the university’s off‑campus housing guidelines [1].\n\n---\n\n**Subject:** Urgent – Heater Repair Needed (Unit # [Your Unit])\n\nDear [Landlord’s Name],\n\nI hope you’re well. I’m writing to let you know that the heater in my unit has not been working since [date – e.g., Monday, September 23]. As the weather is now cold, this is affecting the habitability of the apartment.\n\n**Requested action:** Please arrange for the heater to be repaired by **[reasonable deadline – e.g., Friday, September 27]**. If you need access to the unit, I’m happy to coordinate a convenient time.\n\nI’ve attached (or will provide) any photos/documentation of the issue, and I will keep a copy of this email for my records.\n\nThank you for your prompt attention to this matter. I look forward to hearing from you soon.\n\nKind regards,  \n[Your Full Name]  \n[Your Phone Number – optional]  \n[Your Unit/Address]\n\n---\n\nIf the heater is not fixed by the deadline, the next step is to contact the Landlord and Tenant Board for dispute resolution [1]. If you ever lose heat entirely or face a safety risk, you can also reach out to the province’s Rental Housing Enforcement Unit for immediate assistance.  \n\nGood luck, and stay warm!\n\n_Note: This is general information, not legal advice. For landlord-tenant disputes, contact Waterloo Region Community Legal Services or the Landlord and Tenant Board directly._",
